############ WHAT THE SIGNALS PROVE ############

container ID    unchanged -> same container object, never removed or recreated
Created         unchanged -> never recreated from scratch
StartedAt       unchanged -> PID 1 never exited; the container was never restarted
RestartCount    0         -> Docker's restart counter never incremented
image ID        unchanged -> no 'docker build' was run for this swap
binary SHA      changed   -> the file inside was replaced, and matches the host copy
entrypoint.sh   same PID  -> the supervisor loop kept running throughout

This is a true in-place swap: the supervisor stops the old child and
re-spawns the new binary without ever exiting, so Docker has nothing to restart.
